<BaseLayout title="Alternator" description="Alternator excitation wiring and charging system notes for LS Genesis Coupe swaps.">
  <h1>Alternator</h1>
  <p>
    Most LS alternators need a switched 12V excitation (sense) wire to start charging.
    This is not a plain battery wire — it must include the correct resistor or a purpose-built harness.
  </p>

  <h2>Excitation Wire</h2>
  <p>Options:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Wire into a Holley ECU input that can provide the correct signal, or</li>
    <li>Tap a switched source at the driver's-side fuse box with the proper resistor inline.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A convenient option is the
    <a href="https://www.sloppymechanics.com/products/holley-plug-and-play-flex-fuel-alternator-combo-harness" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sloppy Mechanics Holley plug-and-play flex-fuel / alternator combo harness</a>
    that plugs into the Holley power tap.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/holley-alternator-input.jpg" alt="Holley Terminator X Inputs screen with Flex/Alt on J1-A12 as +12V" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Terminator X Inputs screen — Flex/Alt assigned as type +12V on pin J1-A12 and enabled. Use the I/O icon (circled) to reach this page.
  </p>

  <p>
    Configure the input in Holley software under the Inputs / I/O section. Name it something clear (e.g. Flex/Alt), set type to <strong>+12V</strong>, assign the correct ECU pin for your harness, and enable it.
  </p>

  <h2>Practical Tips</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Confirm charging voltage at the battery with the engine running (typically mid-13s to low 14s V depending on regulator).</li>
    <li>Keep the main charge cable short and properly sized; ground the alternator case solidly to the block and chassis.</li>
    <li>Corvette vs F-body accessory spacing changes bracket and belt routing — choose spacing for radiator clearance first (see Cooling guide).</li>
  </ul>
</BaseLayout>
